  "worked_solution": "Notice that $∠BAF$  and $∠AFC$ are alternate interior angles formed when the transversal $\\overset{↔}{AF}$ cuts the parallel lines $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ and $\\overset{↔}{CF}.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠AFC=m∠BAF={71}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246974/assets/bfb1ce1e2aba3efe901251ad40f84fda062e8324c4dd7a1f2888081724679842.png)\n\nAlso, notice that $∠FBE$  and $∠BFC$ are alternate interior angles formed when the transversal $\\overset{↔}{BF}$ cuts the parallel lines $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ and $\\overset{↔}{CF}.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠BFC=m∠FBE={139}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, using the angle addition postulate, we can find $∠AFB:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AFB & =m∠BFC-m∠AFC \\\\  & ={139}^{∘}-{71}^{∘} \\\\  & ={68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
